A||||3||batboy||Where to buy parts?
Z||000000||batboy||07-18-99||08:32 AM||||I need some advice on where to shop for the following Fiero items:  front battery relocating mounting kit, front air dam, nice floor mats that fit. I like the metal battery bracket from West Coast Fiero, but is there anything like that out there that is less than $120? I really like the looks of FOCOA's DGP air dam for coupes (I have a 85). Has anyone have it? Do you like it, is it good quality? Someplace has to have good floor mats cheaper than the $80 Fiero Store ones. They look nice in the pics, but too much money for something that's gonna get muddy.
Z||000001||Pretender||07-18-99||11:59 PM||||you might have to "bite the bullet" on trhe floor mats, i heard that the fiero store was the only place for the mats. on the battery locating kit they are asking alot for a piece of sheet metal, but i have never seen another like it, you might be able to have a machine shop duplicate the metal work and return the new one. just a thought.
Z||000002||lowCG||07-19-99||12:03 AM||||Hey man,been trying to get some springs this last week to check out and make real sure they're the ones I had before,so as to give you a proper part #,then I got to thinking about that large cast iron mass in the back of your car...<br>What is the weight of your car,and what are the weights of the front,and rear ,when put on the scales separately?<br>Do you know what(rate)springs are in back?<br>Once I get the springs,and put my car together I should know right away if I've got the same rate(I have an alternative set in mind,but they're a couple of steps stiffer,and they'd be uncharted territory for Fiero useage).
Z||000003||batboy||07-19-99||01:40 AM||||LowCG, I don't know the exact weight, my bathroom scales isn't big enough, even for half of a little Fiero. I've heard that the Chevy small block is about 150 pounds heavier than the 2.5 liter. The previous owner said that he put heavier duty springs on the car that came with the conversion kit, but I don't know about the spring rate either. Actually, I'm quite happy with the way the springs feel, but I've got lots of wear in all the suspension bushings and maybe a bit in the ball joints that needs fixed. I was wanting to bring the front nose down a little, but with slightly smaller tires and using your Caddy ball joint trick, I should be about where I want to be (better than an inch lower than before). I don't want to go too low for fear of scraping my future air dam. We grow really big potholes out here in these parts. However, keep me posted, I've been known to change my mind from time to time.
